\(2-2\alpha_{0}^{J}=1\) for double-baryon stopping and \(\alpha_{J}=2-\alpha_{0}^{J}-\alpha_{P}(0)=0.42\) for single-baryon stopping, using \(\alpha\simeq 0.5\), and \(\alpha_{P}(0)=1.08\).

#### Current tests of baryon junction hypothesis

Currently, two approaches have been employed to test the baryon junction hypothesis, i.e., measuring net-proton yields in heavy-ion and \(\gamma\)+A events.

Firstly, authors of Ref.[56] explored the rapidity dependence of the net-proton yield.

